## SECTION A: IDENTITY, SIGNUP, SIGN-IN & RECOVERY

### A1. Sign-in methods (modern, privacy-first)
- ONE button, labeled "Sign in / Sign up", default mode SIGN IN. The same screen handles new
  and existing users (Section F, chart 2). Responses are neutral so nobody can learn whether
  an email or phone number is registered; a new account is created only after the identity is
  proven, in a "complete sign-up" step.
- Primary: passkeys (WebAuthn), phishing-resistant, no password needed.
- Email or phone with country code: one-time verification code (or link) to prove ownership.
  Phone numbers use a country-code picker; email users choose their country code in the
  complete-sign-up step (Section C). Codes are short-lived, single-use, rate-limited.
- Social sign-in: "Continue with Google / Apple / X" using OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect with
  Authorization Code + PKCE, `state` and `nonce` validation, exact redirect-URI matching, and
  server-side token exchange only in the control plane. Social tokens are never stored longer
  than needed and never exposed to the browser beyond the session.
- Optional password login: processed so the server never sees the plaintext (OPAQUE-style
  protocol or client-side key derivation), because heavy server-side hashing may exceed the
  free-plan CPU limit. Passwords are HASHED/derived, never encrypted. Verify feasibility first.
- Minimal data: store only a provider-issued stable user ID or the verified email/phone, plus
  the minimum profile fields I approve. Treat provider-returned email as unverified for X,
  which may not return one.
- Account linking: one account can link several methods, only after re-authentication.
  Prevent duplicate accounts and takeover by an unverified email match.

### A2. No keys in development, keys only by eligible admins
- Development uses MOCK adapters that simulate Google, Apple, X, email and SMS flows,
  including success, cancel, error, delay and "email not provided" cases.
- Real provider credentials are added, rotated or revoked ONLY by an eligible admin (Super
  User or Integrations/Secrets admin) in the console via the vault (Section S).
- Until an admin enables a method, its option stays hidden. If a provider's credentials are
  revoked, that method turns off instantly and users fall back to their other linked methods.
- Be honest with me: Apple needs a paid developer membership, SMS has per-message cost and
  local registration rules, and X's free tier and email availability may change. Show current
  facts and let me decide before building those parts.

### A3. Account and password recovery
- Recovery must not depend on paid providers by default. Offer these layered paths, and I
  choose which to enable:
  1. Another linked social login or another registered passkey/device.
  2. One-time recovery codes generated at signup, shown once, stored only as hashes, and
     regenerable after sign-in.
  3. Admin-assisted recovery: a Support admin verifies identity by a defined checklist, and
     the Super User approves; every step is audit-logged, with a cooling-off delay and a
     notification on every other active session.
  4. Verified email/phone code, available once an eligible admin adds that provider key
     through the vault.
- Password reset (if passwords are enabled): only through a verified recovery path above,
  invalidating all sessions and tokens after reset.
- Anti-takeover rules: rate limits, delay windows for recovery, alerts on new devices, and no
  recovery path that bypasses MFA for admins.
- Recovery for admins and the Super User is stricter: no social-only recovery, and approval by
  a second authorized person where more than one exists.

### A4. Session, MFA and credentials
- Short-lived access tokens, rotating refresh tokens, HttpOnly + Secure + SameSite cookies,
  session/device list with remote logout, brute-force lockout, rate limiting.
- TOTP or passkey MFA mandatory for every admin; optional but encouraged for users.
- Separate login entry and session scope for users, each admin category and the Super User.
  Admins sign in with passkey + MFA, not social, email or phone login.
- Sensitive personal fields are encrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM, envelope keys).
- First Super User: created by a one-time bootstrap process. Credentials are generated then,
  shown ONLY in this chat/terminal once, stored nowhere in plain text, with a forced change on
  first login. Later admin credentials are issued the same way.
- ACCOUNT DELETION: immediately and permanently erase credentials, linked social IDs, email/
  phone, recovery codes, sessions, tokens, preferences and personal data, and revoke
  entitlement tokens. Keep only minimal anonymized payment/tax records if the law requires;
  tell me exactly what applies in India.

## SECTION C: COUNTRY CODE & CURRENCY
- No detection and no selector (Principle 10). Currency is fixed ONCE, at signup:
  - Phone signup: the country code comes from the number.
  - Email or social signup: the user picks a country code in the complete-sign-up step.
- An admin-managed table maps each country code to one currency, plus a fallback currency
  for unmapped codes or currencies the gateway cannot process.
- At signup, show the resulting currency with a short plain-language explanation, for example:
  "Prices will be shown in [currency] because you selected [code]. This is set for your account."
- After signup the currency is read-only everywhere (account page shows it with the same
  explanation). Changes only by an eligible admin, with a reason, MFA re-auth and audit entry.
- Guests see plans and features but no prices; prices appear after sign-in/sign-up
  (confirm in Phase 0, category R).
- Be honest with me: a country code is not proof of residence. Add mitigations: verified phone
  where used, locked currency, admin review flags for unusual patterns, gateway billing checks,
  and tax handling that does not rely on the code alone.

